   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Evidence generated from HVP supported study, evaluation or review contribute to relevant government ministries and the universities knowledge management system</narrative>
    </title>
    <baseline year="2021"></baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-12-31"></period-end>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">HVP has developed a credible government engagement approach at national and regional level. Its main strength is that it works through existing government systems &#x2014; especially Seqota Declaration and Ministry of Health structures. This supports sustainability because programme learning, tools and technical support are being linked to government planning, coordination and scale-up processes.The programme increasingly focused on the Seqota Declaration as the main government platform for multisectoral nutrition coordination and scale-up, compared to its original strategic focus on alignment with several Government of Ethiopia initiatives. The Seqota Declaration has the mandate to convene relevant sectors, so HVP&#x2019;s influence on wider ministries often works indirectly through this platform. At regional level, HVP also engaged more directly in multisectoral coordination, although this was affected by conflict, access constraints and uneven sector participation.The most functional engagement mechanisms were:&#x2022; secondment into government structures;&#x2022; joint planning and review;&#x2022; participation in government-led platforms;&#x2022; co-development of tools, guidance and models;&#x2022; evidence-sharing and digital systems work.These mechanisms helped position HVP as an evidence- and learning-oriented partnership, rather than only an implementation project.The HVP has contributed to several government-facing products and processes, including the WASH&#x2013;Nutrition Nexus guidance, the Malnutrition-Free Healthy Village model/guideline, GMP digitalisation and e-CHIS work, community-based GMP models, and the Seqota Declaration Scaling Curriculum. More recent evidence suggests that HVP has moved beyond simply laying foundations for future uptake. HVP learning is already informing the Seqota Declaration Scale-Up Phase. However, scale-up should not be treated as complete. The main task now is to ensure that tools, models and routines are used consistently in planning, training, supervision, budgeting, implementation and learning across national, regional, woreda and kebele levels.HVP&#x2019;s contribution is best understood as a credible contribution to government uptake, sustainability and scale-up readiness. The quality and sustainability of wider rollout will be affected by including conflict, restricted access, uneven sector participation, government financing, staffing, workload and digital system maintenance. Scale-up will depend on continued government leadership, resourcing, coordination and accountability during the Seqota Declaration Scale-Up Phase.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
